<h1>How to write a title tag</h1>
<p class="dek">The title tag is the blue link people click in search results. Five rules for writing one that earns the click.</p>
<div class="post-meta">
<address>
<ul class="people" role="list" aria-label="Written by">
<li class="person"><span><a rel="author" href="/blog/authors/asif/">Asif</a><span class="person-role">Founder of SerpDad</span></span></li>
</ul>
</address>
<p class="post-date">Published <time datetime="2026-09-21">21 September 2026</time></p>
<dl class="video-facts">
<div>
<dt>Length</dt>
<dd>4:05</dd>
</div>
<div>
<dt>Level</dt>
<dd>Beginner</dd>
</div>
</dl>
</div>
</header>
<div class="post-body">
<figure class="video">
<video controls preload="none" poster="/media/title-tags.jpg" aria-label="Writing a title tag"><source src="/media/title-tags.mp4" /><a href="/media/title-tags.mp4">Writing a title tag</a></video>
<figcaption>Writing a title tag</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The title tag is the line of text a search engine shows as the clickable headline of your result. It is often the first thing a reader sees of your site, so it decides whether they visit at all.</p>
<h2 id="five-rules">Five rules <a class="heading-link" href="#five-rules" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Put the topic first.</strong> &quot;Title tags: how to write one&quot; beats &quot;A complete and friendly guide to title tags&quot;.</li>
<li><strong>Keep it under about 60 characters,</strong> so it is not cut off on a phone.</li>
<li><strong>Make it match the page.</strong> If the title promises a checklist, the page needs a checklist. A mismatch sends readers straight back.</li>
<li><strong>Write one per page.</strong> Two pages with the same title compete with each other.</li>
<li><strong>Add your brand at the end, if there is room.</strong> &quot;Title tags: how to write one | Field Notes&quot;.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="title-tag-or-headline">Title tag or headline? <a class="heading-link" href="#title-tag-or-headline" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>They can differ. The headline on the page can be longer and warmer. The title tag is for the results page, where space is short and the reader is scanning.</p>
<p>When the title is done, check that the page is easy to reach: <a href="/blog/why-internal-links-matter/">why internal links matter</a> explains how.</p>

</div>
